  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how antioxidant nanozymes can protect different tissues from reactive oxygen species (ROS) generated by drugs, radiation, chemicals, or pollution. At the top left, ROS are shown being converted into less harmful products by enzyme-like activities such as SOD, catalase, and GPx, establishing the overall mechanism.\n\nThe central human silhouette links routes of delivery and target organs. One strategy is oral administration to protect gastrointestinal stem cells. Another is systemic injection, including an MSC membrane-coated Prussian blue nanozyme, which appears to target stem-cell-related injury in the gastrointestinal tract and blood-forming system.\n\nThe organ-specific examples emphasize localized ROS scavenging. In the eye, UV and air pollution increase ROS at the ocular surface, while a contact-lens nanozyme lowers oxidative stress; a CoFe/NiFe/FeFe composition is indicated. In the liver, acetaminophen-induced oxidative damage is reduced by mesoporous nanozymes, with smaller particles loaded into mesoporous silica seeming to improve delivery. In the kidney, injury from cisplatin or glycerol is countered by ultra-small coated nanozymes; the panel suggests that very small size and surface coatings such as PEG, PVP, or chitosan enhance activity. Overall, the figure highlights nanozymes as ROS-detoxifying therapeutics across multiple organs.",
